    {
      "children": [
        {
          "position": [
            76,
            378
          ],
          "type": "text",
          "value": "A facet is not four charts. It is one chart cut into panels over the values of\na field, and the panels share their scales — which is the whole reason the\nform works, because a shared scale is what makes two panels comparable at a\nglance rather than four separate y-axes that happen to sit side by side."
        }
      ],
      "position": [
        76,
        378
      ],
      "type": "paragraph"
    },
    {
      "children": [
        {
          "position": [
            380,
            396
          ],
          "type": "inlineCode",
          "value": "column: region"
        },
        {
          "position": [
            396,
            417
          ],
          "type": "text",
          "value": " cuts by region, and "
        },
        {
          "position": [
            417,
            429
          ],
          "type": "inlineCode",
          "value": "columns: 2"
        },
        {
          "position": [
            429,
            683
          ],
          "type": "text",
          "value": " wraps the four panels into a\ntwo-by-two grid instead of one long row. Panel order follows first appearance\nin the data, the same rule series identity uses, so filtering the data cannot\nreshuffle the grid underneath a reader who has learnt where to look."
        }
      ],
      "position": [
